Assignment-5 : SQL Solutions
(Using Oracle's default EMP and DEPT tables)
EMP(EMPNO, ENAME, JOB, MGR, HIREDATE, SAL, COMM, DEPTNO)
DEPT(DEPTNO, DNAME, LOC)
=====================================================================

1. List all the employee names, dept name and the city, in department
   name order.
--------------------------------------
SELECT E.ENAME, D.DNAME, D.LOC
FROM EMP E
JOIN DEPT D ON E.DEPTNO = D.DEPTNO
ORDER BY D.DNAME;


2. List all employees working in Dallas in descending order of salary.
--------------------------------------
SELECT E.*
FROM EMP E
JOIN DEPT D ON E.DEPTNO = D.DEPTNO
WHERE D.LOC = 'DALLAS'
ORDER BY E.SAL DESC;


3. List employee name, department name, job and location of all
   employees who work in DALLAS.
--------------------------------------
SELECT E.ENAME, D.DNAME, E.JOB, D.LOC
FROM EMP E
JOIN DEPT D ON E.DEPTNO = D.DEPTNO
WHERE D.LOC = 'DALLAS';


4. List the employee name, salary, PF, HRA, DA and gross salary;
   order the result in ascending order of gross. PF is 10% of salary,
   HRA is 60% of salary and DA is 40% of salary.
--------------------------------------
SELECT ENAME,
       SAL,
       SAL * 0.10 AS PF,
       SAL * 0.60 AS HRA,
       SAL * 0.40 AS DA,
       SAL + (SAL * 0.60) + (SAL * 0.40) AS GROSS
FROM EMP
ORDER BY GROSS ASC;


5. Display names and salary of all the employees who report to KING.
--------------------------------------
SELECT ENAME, SAL
FROM EMP
WHERE MGR = (SELECT EMPNO FROM EMP WHERE ENAME = 'KING');


6. List all employees who work in DALLAS and earn more than any
   employee working in Chicago.
--------------------------------------
SELECT *
FROM EMP
WHERE DEPTNO IN (SELECT DEPTNO FROM DEPT WHERE LOC = 'DALLAS')
  AND SAL > ANY (
        SELECT SAL FROM EMP
        WHERE DEPTNO IN (SELECT DEPTNO FROM DEPT WHERE LOC = 'CHICAGO')
      );


7. List all employees who work in the same post as Smith.
--------------------------------------
SELECT *
FROM EMP
WHERE JOB = (SELECT JOB FROM EMP WHERE ENAME = 'SMITH')
  AND ENAME <> 'SMITH';


8. Find the job with the highest average salary.
--------------------------------------
SELECT JOB, AVG(SAL) AS avg_salary
FROM EMP
GROUP BY JOB
HAVING AVG(SAL) = (
    SELECT MAX(avg_sal)
    FROM (SELECT AVG(SAL) AS avg_sal FROM EMP GROUP BY JOB)
);


9. List the top 10 earners in the company.
--------------------------------------
SELECT *
FROM (SELECT * FROM EMP ORDER BY SAL DESC)
WHERE ROWNUM <= 10;


10. Display the names of all employees' replacing 'A' with 'a'.
--------------------------------------
SELECT REPLACE(ENAME, 'A', 'a') AS ENAME
FROM EMP;


11. Show the salary of all the employees rounding it to the nearest
    Rs.1000/-.
--------------------------------------
SELECT ENAME, ROUND(SAL, -3) AS SAL
FROM EMP;


12. Show the first three and last three characters of the names of
    all the employees.
--------------------------------------
SELECT SUBSTR(ENAME, 1, 3) AS first_three,
       SUBSTR(ENAME, -3) AS last_three
FROM EMP;


Notes
=====================================================================
- Q6 uses "> ANY", which in SQL means greater than at least one value
  in the Chicago salary list (not necessarily the highest). If your
  course intends "greater than every Chicago employee" instead, use
  "> ALL" in place of "> ANY".
- Q8's subquery avoids nested aggregates (MAX(AVG(...)) is not valid
  directly in Oracle); it wraps the grouped averages in an inline
  view first.
- Q9 uses the classic ROWNUM pattern for "top N" rows, which works
  across Oracle versions. If you're on Oracle 12c or later, you can
  instead write: ... ORDER BY SAL DESC FETCH FIRST 10 ROWS ONLY.